@page "/charts"

<DocShell Title="Charts" Tracks="G.Tracks" Trail="G.Trail" Portrait="Portrait" PortraitCaption="Plan mix">
    <Lede>
        <code>DaisyBlazor.Charts</code> draws 50 chart types as inline SVG laid out by a D3 module
        bundled in the package. No ApexCharts, no Chart.js, nothing to install from npm, and every colour is a daisyUI theme token.
    </Lede>
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="prose-doc">
            <p>
                Every chart is rendered as <strong>inline <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code></strong> whose geometry (scales, ticks, paths, arcs) is computed by the D3 module bundled in the package, and every colour is
                emitted as a daisyUI theme token (<code>var(--color-primary)</code>, …), so charts <strong>follow the active
                <code>data-theme</code> automatically</strong>, with no recompilation. The only dependency is <code>DaisyBlazor.Components</code>
                (for the <code>Color</code> enum and the <code>CssClass</code> merge helper).
            </p>
            <p>
                The charts need <strong>JavaScript</strong>: the module (<code>_content/DaisyBlazor.Charts/charts.mjs</code>) loads on first use.
                Until it answers, each chart shows a placeholder of the same size, so nothing jumps. To send the finished chart in the
                first HTML (prerendering, static server rendering), add the server package described at the end of this guide.
            </p>

            @H(0)
            <CodeBlock Language="bash" Code="dotnet add package DaisyBlazor.Charts" />
            <CodeBlock Title="_Imports.razor" Code="@@using DaisyBlazor.Charts" />

            @H(1)
            <p>Each name opens the chart's page, with live examples, their code and the full parameter list.</p>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (ComponentDoc doc in DocRegistry.All.Where(d => d.Category == Category.Charts))
                    {
                        <tr><td><a href="@($"components/{doc.Slug}")"><code>@doc.Name</code></a></td><td>@FirstSentence(doc.Summary)</td></tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>
                Every chart also accepts <code>Title</code> (heading above the chart, also the accessible label), <code>Width</code> (default
                <code>"100%"</code>), <code>Height</code> (default <code>"16rem"</code>; <code>Sparkline</code> <code>"1.5rem"</code>,
                <code>Width</code> <code>"5rem"</code>), and the standard <code>Class</code> / <code>Style</code> / unmatched-attribute splat.
            </p>

            @H(2)
            <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@Models" />
            <p>
                <code>ChartSeries.Data</code> aligns by <strong>ordinal index</strong> with <code>Labels</code>. <code>ChartDataPoint.Value</code>
                must be finite; for the circular charts, non-positive values are ignored.
            </p>

            @H(3)
            <p>
                Colours come from <code>ChartPalette</code>, which returns daisyUI tokens as CSS variables. Charts index into it (wrapping with
                modulo) so each series or slice gets a distinct, theme-driven colour. The default cycling order, in the current theme:
            </p>
        </div>
        <ol class="mt-5 flex max-w-[70ch] flex-wrap gap-2" aria-label="Default chart palette">
            @foreach (string c in ChartPalette.Default)
            {
                <li class="flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-base-200 py-1 pl-1 pr-3 text-sm font-semibold">
                    <span class="size-6 rounded-full" style="background:@c"></span>@(c[12..^1])
                </li>
            }
        </ol>
        <div class="prose-doc mt-5">
            <p>
                Because the values are <code>var(--color-*)</code>, switching the daisyUI theme re-skins every chart instantly, with no rebuild and no new layout.
                Override the colour of an individual series or point by setting its <code>Color</code> to <strong>any CSS colour</strong>:
            </p>
            <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@Overrides" />
            <p>
                <code>ChartPalette.FromColor(Color)</code> also maps the DaisyBlazor <code>Color</code> enum to its token, so you can drive chart
                colours from the same semantic enum used by the components.
            </p>

            @H(4)
            <p>Rendered live, then the code. Switch to another theme from the top bar and watch the colours follow.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-5 grid max-w-[70ch] gap-6 rounded-2xl border border-base-300 bg-base-100 p-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <LineChart Title="Weekly visitors" Labels="@Days" Series="@_traffic" Smooth Area Class="sm:col-span-2" />
            <BarChart Title="Signups by plan" Data="@_bars" Height="12rem" />
            <DonutChart Title="Visits by source" Data="@_breakdown" CenterLabel="100" Height="12rem" />
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 sm:col-span-2">
                <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Sparkline</span>
                <Sparkline Data="@(new double[] { 3, 5, 4, 8, 6, 9 })" Area />
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose-doc mt-5">
            <CodeBlock Code="@Usage" />

            @H(5)
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Line, area, bar charts and sparklines are laid out at the real pixel size of their box (<code>Width</code> by <code>Height</code>)
                    and again when it is resized, so text and strokes are drawn at true size. Pie and donut charts scale a square to their <code>Height</code>.
                </li>
                <li>Value axes include a <strong>zero baseline</strong> and round out to nice ticks (large values read <code>50k</code>); flat or empty data is handled without divide-by-zero, and a missing value leaves a gap.</li>
                <li><code>BarChart</code> takes <code>Series</code> (grouped) over <code>Data</code> (flat) when both are supplied; <code>Horizontal</code> flips the axis.</li>
                <li>
                    Pie and donut slices come from <code>d3.pie</code> and <code>d3.arc</code>, in data order from 12 o'clock, clockwise.
                    Only positive values contribute to the total.
                </li>
                <li><code>Smooth</code> line mode uses monotone cubic curves (<code>d3.curveMonotoneX</code>), which never overshoot the data.</li>
                <li>Every chart sets <code>role="img"</code> and an accessible <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> (from <code>Title</code> when provided).</li>
            </ul>

            @H(6)
            <p>
                <code>DaisyBlazor.Charts.Server</code> runs the same layout files on the server with <a href="https://github.com/sebastienros/jint">Jint</a>,
                a JavaScript interpreter written in .NET, so no Node.js is needed. Register it once and every chart computes its layout before its first
                render: prerendered and statically rendered pages contain the finished SVG instead of a placeholder. The prerendered layout travels to the
                interactive render with the persisted component state, so a WebAssembly chart keeps showing it while it starts; charts sized by their
                box (line, area, bar, sparkline) then lay out again at its real size. The <code>dotnet new daisyblazor</code> template comes with it registered.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock Language="bash" Code="dotnet add package DaisyBlazor.Charts.Server" />
            <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Language="csharp" Code="builder.Services.AddDaisyBlazorChartsServerRendering();" />
            <p>
                The first layout of each kind parses the D3 modules it needs (a few hundred milliseconds, once per engine); most layouts then take under 5 ms, force-directed networks and density
                contours longer, and a chart with the same data is answered from a cache. The server and browser results are tested to match for every
                chart type.
            </p>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
</DocShell>

@code {
    private static readonly GuideDoc G = Guides.Get("charts");

    private static string FirstSentence(string text)
    {
        int end = text.IndexOf(". ", StringComparison.Ordinal);
        return end < 0 ? text : text[..(end + 1)];
    }

    private static RenderFragment H(int i) => __builder =>
    {
        <h2 id="@G.Tracks[i].Id" class="scroll-mt-6 font-display">@G.Tracks[i].Title</h2>
    };

    private static readonly string[] Days = ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri"];

    private readonly ChartSeries[] _traffic =
    [
        new("Visitors", new double[] { 30, 40, 35, 50, 49 }),
        new("Signups", new double[] { 5, 8, 6, 12, 10 }),
    ];

    private readonly ChartDataPoint[] _bars = [new("Starter", 12), new("Pro", 28), new("Team", 19)];

    private readonly ChartDataPoint[] _breakdown = [new("Direct", 40), new("Search", 35), new("Social", 25)];

    private readonly ChartDataPoint[] _plans = [new("Free", 48), new("Pro", 35), new("Enterprise", 17)];

    private RenderFragment Portrait => __builder =>
    {
        <div class="space-y-3">
            <DonutChart Data="@_plans" CenterLabel="1,240" ShowLegend="false" Height="9.5rem" Title="Customers by plan" />
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-sm">
                <div class="rounded-md bg-base-200 p-2">
                    <span class="block font-semibold">Signups</span>
                    <Sparkline Data="@(new double[] { 4, 6, 5, 9, 7, 11 })" Area Width="100%" />
                </div>
                <div class="rounded-md bg-base-200 p-2">
                    <span class="block font-semibold">Churn</span>
                    <Sparkline Data="@(new double[] { 8, 7, 9, 6, 8, 5 })" Color="var(--color-secondary)" Width="100%" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    };

    private const string Models = """
        // A single categorical point — used by BarChart (flat), PieChart, DonutChart.
        // Color is an optional per-point CSS override.
        public record ChartDataPoint(string Label, double Value, string? Color = null);

        // A named numeric series — used by LineChart, AreaChart, grouped BarChart.
        // Data index i lines up with the chart's X label i.
        public class ChartSeries
        {
            public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;
            public IReadOnlyList<double> Data { get; set; } = new List<double>();
            public string? Color { get; set; }              // optional CSS override

            public ChartSeries() { }
            public ChartSeries(string name, IReadOnlyList<double> data, string? color = null) { /* … */ }
        }
        """;

    private const string Overrides = """
        new ChartSeries("Errors", data, color: "var(--color-error)");   // a theme token
        new ChartDataPoint("Other", 12, "#94a3b8");                     // a hex literal
        """;

    private const string Usage = """
        @using DaisyBlazor.Charts

        <LineChart Title="Weekly visitors"
                   Labels="@(new[] { "Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri" })"
                   Series="@_traffic"
                   Smooth Area />

        <BarChart Title="Signups by plan"
                  Data="@(new[]
                  {
                      new ChartDataPoint("Starter", 12),
                      new ChartDataPoint("Pro", 28),
                      new ChartDataPoint("Team", 19)
                  })" />

        <DonutChart Title="Visits by source" Data="@_breakdown" CenterLabel="100" />

        <Sparkline Data="@(new double[] { 3, 5, 4, 8, 6, 9 })" Area />

        @code {
            private readonly ChartSeries[] _traffic =
            {
                new("Visitors", new double[] { 30, 40, 35, 50, 49 }),
                new("Signups",  new double[] {  5,  8,  6, 12, 10 })
            };

            private readonly ChartDataPoint[] _breakdown =
            {
                new("Direct", 40),
                new("Search", 35),
                new("Social", 25)
            };
        }
        """;
}
